    {'id':'fence-api-and-numbers','kind':'software','status':'verified','statement':'The original two-row, three-column fence selects largest k entries along the expert axis and returns sorted values plus original integer column indices without normalization or expert execution; displayed sums and both exercises are correct. Tied indices need not be stable.','location':'course/chapters/15.md original fence lines126-133 and paragraphs136,142','scope':'Grouped API coverage: torch.tensor, Tensor.topk(k,dim=-1), Tensor.sum(-1), Tensor.tolist, topk defaults/tie contract; exact integer choices for distinct input values, float32 value/sum tolerance1e-7. Fence has no backward, expert call or parameter update. Binary approximation principle applies, without treating Python float53-bit examples as tensor dtype claims. No cross-device tie behavior was measured.','evidence':[evidence('torch-docs','torch.tensor9582-9635; torch.sum11266-11321; torch.topk11877-11921','Construction, reduction on the requested axis, largest/sorted defaults, returned values/indices and documented unstable tied indices.'),evidence('tensor-docs','Tensor.sum5027-5034; Tensor.tolist5445-5465; Tensor.topk5467-5474','Method contracts and conversion to nested ordinary Python lists.'),evidence('floatingpoint','floatingpoint.rst original lines16-27,46-62,90-95','Finite binary representations approximate many decimal fractions; display may expose nearby decimals.'),evidence('cpu-check','verification-results.json /topk_axes_values_indices; original-fence.stdout.txt','Unchanged original fence executed; row swap and k3 independently checked with numerical tolerance.')],'artifact_ids':['cpu-results','original-fence-output','fence-code','primary-torch','primary-tensor','primary-floatingpoint'],'verification':{'method':'executed','expected':'k2 indices[[1,2],[0,1]], sums[.9,.9]; k1 indices[[1],[0]], sums[.7,.6]; first-row swap gives[2,1] and[.7,.2]; k3 retains all three with sum1.','observed':'All assertions pass; actual float32 sums .8999999761581421/.9000000357627869 and .699999988079071/.6000000238418579; row-swap and k3 match.','details':'Executed the original280-byte fence without modifications, then bounded CPU variants. Values, axes, shape(2,k), integer indices and no normalization checked together.','tolerance':'Integer indices exact for distinct values; float32 absolute tolerance1e-7, zero relative tolerance.'}},
    {'id':'selected-value-gradient','kind':'concept','status':'verified','statement':'The expert identity is an integer choice that changes at ranking boundaries; ordinary autograd does not differentiate those indices. Retained softmax values can propagate a local gradient to router scores while the chosen set stays fixed.','location':'course/chapters/15.md line138','scope':'Local selected-value derivative away from ties and ranking boundaries, with the current unnormalized probability convention. Does not assert nonzero gradients for every loss, or normalized top1; does not prove a trained router learned useful choices.','evidence':[evidence('derivatives','tools/autograd/derivatives.yaml1791-1795','topk values are differentiable, indices are not; the backward routes selected value gradients.'),evidence('shazeer2017','p4 section2.1 Training the Gating Network and preceding discontinuity paragraph','Original sparse gate can have discontinuities at selection changes while selected continuous gates permit backpropagation; original normalized k>1 condition remains distinct.'),evidence('cpu-check','verification-results.json /gradient_local_continuity','k1 and k2 raw retained softmax weighted sums have autograd gradients matching fixed-set finite differences; crossing the rank boundary switches indices.')],'artifact_ids':['cpu-results','primary-derivatives','primary-shazeer']},
    {'id':'repository-sparse-execution','kind':'software','status':'verified','statement':'The repository MoEFFN actually chooses before dispatch and only calls an expert on its selected token rows. A token can select different experts from another token in the same batch.','location':'course/chapters/15.md lines121,123,140; linked actual implementation tiny_perceptron/modern.py56-84','scope':'Implementation inspection plus a two-token/four-expert CPU forward-hook probe. Verifies input-row work and skipped unselected experts; gives no training, GPU performance or wall-time improvement claim.','evidence':[evidence('modern','MoEFFN.forward67-84, especially70-79','topk precedes expert evaluation; token_rows/slots from chosen==i, conditional execution, weighted contributions and row restoration.'),evidence('cpu-check','verification-results.json /real_sparse_execution','Controlled router choices produce2 expert input rows for top1 and4 for top2, compared with8 if every expert processed both tokens; fourth expert never called.')],'artifact_ids':['cpu-results','modern-dispatch-code'],'verification':{'method':'executed','expected':'top1 chosen[[1],[0]], rows[1,1,0,0]; top2 chosen[[1,2],[0,1]], rows[1,2,1,0]; output shape[2,2].','observed':'Forward pre-hooks show the expected selected rows and zero calls for unselected experts, including expert3 in both cases. Output shape preserved.','details':'Actual imported MoEFFN;4 experts, width2, hidden3, controlled router matrix. CPU/eval/no_grad, no optimizer and no parameter update. Source SHA agrees with original recorded implementation.'}},
